<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aquileia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aquileia is a town near the head of the Adriatic in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, founded as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony in 181 BCE to guard the north-eastern approaches to Italy. It became one of the largest cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and a major church centre, with fine floor mosaics in its basilica. Sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> in 452, it gave its name to the powerful Patriarchate of Aquileia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aquileia.jpg" alt="Aquileia" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThePhotografer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_Patriarcale_(Aquileia)_-_Exterior.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
